    "question": "Use the formula $\\cos(A + B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B$ to prove that\n$\\cos 2\\theta = \\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta$.",
    "level": "higher",
    "marks": 10,
    "workedSolution": {
     "steps": [
      {
       "title": "Start from the compound angle formula",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "$\\cos(A+B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B$ is true for any angles, so we may choose $A = B = \\theta$."
        },
        {
         "tex": "\\cos(\\theta + \\theta) &= \\cos\\theta \\cos\\theta - \\sin\\theta \\sin\\theta",
         "sym": "Eq(cos(theta+theta), cos(theta)*cos(theta)-sin(theta)*sin(theta))"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Simplify both sides",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "$\\theta + \\theta = 2\\theta$, and the products are squares."
        },
        {
         "tex": "\\cos 2\\theta &= \\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta",
         "sym": "Eq(cos(2*theta), cos(theta)**2-sin(theta)**2)"
        },
        {
         "text": "So $\\cos 2\\theta = \\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta$, as required."
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "finalAnswer": "\\cos 2\\theta = \\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta"
    },
    "finalSym": "0",
    "answerSym": "simplify(expand_trig(cos(2*theta)) - (cos(theta)**2 - sin(theta)**2))"
   },
